  "notes": "West leads the ♥K against 4♠. West cashes 2 top hearts and leads a third round, East ruffing. East's diamond return is won by South with the ♦A. It seems that the contract depends on the club finesse working.\n\nWest is almost certain to have the ♦K. West's takeout double makes it very likely that he has that card. As a further inference, if East had the ♦K he would probably have switched to a trump instead of a diamond away from the ♦K.\n\nAssuming West has the ♦K, South can make the contract without finessing in clubs by running his winners. On the lead of the ♥Q West will be forced to come down to 2 clubs. The ♣Q will drop under the ♣A and ♣K.\n\nThe advantage of playing this way is that the contract will make when East has a doubleton ♣Q. On the ♥Q, West will have to come down to 2 clubs. South will know that the club finesse cannot gain. The ♣A and ♣K will drop East's doubleton ♣Q.\n\nThe above position is known as a show up squeeze. A show up squeeze will make a finesse unnecessary and will allow declarer to have an extra chance of dropping an offside honour when the finesse was losing.",
